### P1  GIUGA'S CONJECTURE
Statement (Giuga 1950; CITED): n>=2 is prime iff  sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i^(n-1) = -1 (mod n).
Equivalence (Borwein-Borwein-Borwein-Girgensohn, Amer. Math. Monthly 103 (1996); CITED):
  composite n satisfies the congruence iff n is squarefree and for every prime p | n:
  (p-1) | (n/p - 1)  [Carmichael/Korselt]  and  p | (n/p - 1)  [Giuga].
S1 FINITE-EXACT: direct sum computed for every n <= 30000.
    every prime gives -1 (Fermat); composites with sum = -1 mod n: none
    disagreements between the direct congruence and the BBBG criterion on composites: 0
S2 PROVED (re-derivation of BBBG, elementary): with q=p^a || n and k=n-1>=a,
    sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i^k = (n/q) * sum_{i=0}^{q-1} i^k (mod q); the non-units contribute 0 (k>=a).
    a=1: sum of units i^k = -1 if (p-1)|k else 0 (mod p)  [primitive root].
    a>=2, p odd: units = C_{p-1} x (1+pZ), k coprime to p permutes 1+pZ, whose sum is p^(a-1) mod p^a,
      so the unit sum is 0 mod p^(a-1), never -1.  p=2, a>=2: k odd permutes the units, sum = 2^(2a-2) = 0 mod 2^a.
    Hence the congruence forces n squarefree and, for each p|n, (p-1)|(n-1) and n/p = 1 (mod p),
    i.e. (p-1)|(n/p-1) [since n-1 = n/p-1 mod p-1] and p|(n/p-1).  QED (this is BBBG's theorem).
    lemma tables brute-forced: primes p<200 all k<p, prime powers q=p^a<=3000 (a>=2): failures = 0
S3 FINITE-EXACT (n <= 100000): Carmichael numbers: 16, namely [561, 1105, 1729, 2465, 2821, 6601, 8911, 10585, 15841, 29341, 41041, 46657, 52633, 62745, 63973, 75361]
    Giuga numbers (squarefree, p | n/p-1 for all p|n): [30, 858, 1722, 66198]
      30 = 2*3*5,  sum 1/p - 1/n = 1
      858 = 2*3*11*13,  sum 1/p - 1/n = 1
      1722 = 2*3*7*41,  sum 1/p - 1/n = 1
      66198 = 2*3*11*17*59,  sum 1/p - 1/n = 1
    composites satisfying BBBG (= Carmichael AND Giuga): none
    composites satisfying the user's 'p^2(p-1) | n-p for all p|n': none
    composite n <= 100000 where the user's criterion and BBBG disagree: 0; all primes < 2000 satisfy the user's criterion (n-p=0): True
S4 PROVED: the user's criterion is EQUIVALENT to BBBG.  n-p = p(n/p-1), so p^2(p-1) | n-p  iff
    p(p-1) | n/p-1  iff  p | n/p-1 and (p-1) | n/p-1  (gcd(p,p-1)=1).  Squarefreeness is implied:
    if p^2 | n then p | n/p, so p does not divide n/p-1.  For prime n the criterion is vacuous (n-p=0).
    So the paste's restatement is correct (it is the BBBG criterion in one line), and no map to Collatz is involved.
